--- CRITERION 2: TestResolveTransferWritePathRevalidatesBeforeWriting exists, drives the WRITE
    path, asserts refusal AND that transfer_decisions.json is byte-unchanged ---
MET. Read in full at internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/resolve_test.go:245-299.
`grep -c "^func Test" internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/resolve_test.go` = 11;
`git show HEAD:internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/resolve_test.go | grep -c "^func Test"` = 10.
Exactly one test added.
Body, verified by reading not by name:
  resolve_test.go:257  writeCSV(t, dir, transferDecisionsFile, `{"decisions": {}}`)  (baseline bytes)
  resolve_test.go:259  key := seedSuspectedPair(...)  -- seeds CSVs WITHOUT loading through deps
  resolve_test.go:263  mints a real token via the registered tool while the pair IS suspected
  resolve_test.go:272  writeCSV(t, dir, "checking.csv", header only) -- pair stops being suspected
  resolve_test.go:280  _, out, err := applyResolveTransfer(deps, key, transfers.VerdictConfirm,
                       resolveTokenArgs{...}, first.ConfirmToken, confirm.NotAsked)
It calls applyResolveTransfer DIRECTLY, so the top-of-handler pre-mint check at resolve.go:95 is not
in the call path at all and cannot be what refuses -- exactly the primary verifier's prescription.
Assertions present:
  :282-284  err == nil  -> t.Fatal("applyResolveTransfer did not refuse a pair that is no longer
            suspected")
  :285-287  !strings.Contains(err.Error(), "no longer a suspected transfer") -> t.Errorf
  :288-290  out.Confirmed -> t.Error
  :292-298  os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(dir, transferDecisionsFile)); string(after) != before ->
            t.Errorf("transfer_decisions.json changed after a refused write: ...")
Both the refusal AND the byte-unchanged file assertion are present.
The byte-unchanged assertion is NOT vacuous: I proved the write really lands at that path. In the
mutated scratch copy (criterion 3) I added a throwaway diagnostic (scratch only) running the same
sequence and logged the outcome:
  "DIAG err=<nil> confirmed=true snapshots=[.../snapshots/transfer_decisions.json...bak]"
  "DIAG decisions file err=<nil> content=\"{\\n  \\\"decisions\\\": {\\n    \\\"f2dd5ca97cb3\\\": ...
   \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"confirm\\\" ...\" changed=true"
So without the guard the file at dir/transfer_decisions.json is genuinely rewritten; the assertion
would fire. In the committed test the t.Fatal on the missing refusal simply reaches the failure one
assertion earlier, which is correct ordering.

